Translation patch: request_state_wire refactor of the request-state codec (plan Section 9,
"Refactor"; D2 fallback of plan Section 8, "Gates", human decision 2026-11-23)

Applies to: upstream origin/main 7caec762799bece6fba38e4298b8ca7db8bac12c (the SDK Rust sources,
every *.rs file, stay byte-identical to it; only crates/rmcp/Cargo.toml differs, by the Charon
metadata that cargo ignores). Applied only to the copy in .llbc/work/ by
scripts/apply-patches.sh (patch -p1 -F 0, file-name order); the real source is never patched.
Files: crates/rmcp/src/model.rs, crates/rmcp/src/model/request_state.rs,
crates/rmcp/src/model/request_state_wire.rs (new). Generated with git diff 7caec762 ccaf9ee --
crates/rmcp/src/model.rs crates/rmcp/src/model/request_state.rs \
crates/rmcp/src/model/request_state_wire.rs
(ccaf9ee on docs/verification-guides holds the refactor of 9b030c6 plus the S16 tests of
317ace2). The patch body below is the authoritative content. The Charon settings
([package.metadata.charon] in
crates/rmcp/Cargo.toml) are not part of this patch; they stay in the repository.

Change:
- New public module rmcp::model::request_state_wire (W01-W15 of plan Section 2.1: b64_len,
  key_length_ok, check_kid_len, bytes_eq, next_dot, component_count, version_of, parse_rs1,
  parse_rs2, transcript_v1, transcript_v2, decode_expiry, is_expired, open_frame, frame; the
  public constants and the #[non_exhaustive] types KidError, Version, FrameError, Rs1Parts,
  Rs2Parts) and the opaque primitives request_state_wire::prim (hmac_sha256,
  hmac_sha256_verify, b64_encode_into, b64_decode), declared in model.rs under the
  request-state feature.
- request_state.rs is rebuilt on it: open_at uses W07; open_rs1_at/open_rs2_at use W08/W09;
  mac_v1/mac_v2 are deleted and seal/open MAC the W10/W11 transcripts through
  prim::hmac_sha256(_verify); URL_SAFE_NO_PAD calls become prim::b64_*; open_authenticated_body
  uses W14 and seal_at uses W15; validate_key_length/validate_config_kid use W02/W03; the rs1
  fallback loop moves into the private helper rs1_fallbacks_verify (E09a); the codec constants
  come from wire, with the four recorded exceptions of plan Section 2.1.
- Tests (#[cfg(test)] only, part of this patch): the base64/hmac/sha2 imports and the HmacSha256
  alias move into mod tests (use super::* becomes use super::{wire::{..}, *}); one existing
  test (in the rs2 short-body case) computes its tag with prim::hmac_sha256 on W11 instead of
  the deleted mac_v2; all 36 existing request_state tests are kept and no other line of them
  changes. Added: codec_constants_agree_with_wire, mod byte_identity (run-3 token matrix,
  9 tokens and 162 open results, and pinned tokens with their open results incl. Expired and
  InvalidKeyId), the W01-W15 / RFC 4231 / RFC 4648 tests of request_state_wire's mod tests
  (S04a), and the s16_* plan Section 7 boundary-case tests in both files (S16).

Why: Aeneas nightly-2026.08.20-5d08da4 (with its Charon) cannot translate the unpatched codec
(plan Section 9, blocker table; runs 1-2 of the pilot):
- B1: hmac/sha2 types (hybrid_array::Array, mutually recursive with the ArraySize GAT) in the
  signatures of mac_v1/mac_v2 and their callers; opaque still translates signatures.
- B2: str::split crashes Aeneas (core::str::pattern::Pattern has a GAT); used by open_at,
  open_rs1_at, open_rs2_at.
- B3: the generated base64 Engine instance for GeneralPurpose is self-referential.
- B5: Aeneas finds no loop fixed point in open_rs1_at (the fallback loop).
- B6: the pinned library types i64::from_be_bytes wrongly (used by open_authenticated_body);
  W12 uses u64::from_be_bytes(..) as i64 instead.
- B4: returning a &'static str literal fails ("no bottoms in the value"); validate_config_kid
  now gets a KidError from W03 and maps it to the KID_EMPTY_MSG/KID_TOO_LONG_MSG constants.
The code also follows the rules for B7, B8 and B11 (plan Section 9).
With this patch all 25 targets (W01-W15, E01-E04, E07-E11, E09a) translate with real bodies.
Upstream Aeneas main still has B1 (plan Section 9, "Upstream"); this sprint stays on the pinned
release.

Behaviour (argument of the plan Section 9 "Refactor" table):
- request_state_wire: pure functions; the only consumer in the SDK is the codec.
- Codec constants from wire: same values; checked by codec_constants_agree_with_wire and
  tag-length checks against wire::TAG_LEN.
- open_at via W07: the same first '.'-component comparison as before.
- open_rs1_at/open_rs2_at via W08/W09: None exactly when the original part checks returned
  MalformedFormat; component equality argued in plan Section 3.
- mac_v1/mac_v2 replaced by prim::hmac_sha256(_verify) on W10/W11: the same bytes are MACed
  (plan Section 3; tested); verify still uses hmac's constant-time verify_slice (by inspection,
  request_state_wire.rs prim::hmac_sha256_verify); errors are returned in the same order. The
  transcript is now built as one Vec of about the size of associated data plus body before
  MACing (upstream streamed it into the MAC), and seal_at no longer pre-sizes with
  with_capacity: an extra allocation; allocation failure is outside the modelled behaviour.
- URL_SAFE_NO_PAD calls replaced by prim::b64_*: same engine and calls; decode errors still map
  to InvalidEncoding.
- open_authenticated_body via W14, seal_at via W15: same framing and expiry comparison; W12
  (u64::from_be_bytes(head) as i64) is bit-identical to i64::from_be_bytes.
- validate_* via W02/W03: same conditions, same error values and messages.
- rs1_fallbacks_verify: loop body unchanged; every fallback is still tried (by inspection: the
  loop has no early exit; not observable by a functional test); the empty-fallback check still
  comes before decoding (tested).
- The public API is unchanged except for the addition of rmcp::model::request_state_wire
  (rustdoc JSON comparison); RequestStateCodec::MIN_KEY_LENGTH keeps its value 32 but rustdoc
  now shows it as wire::MIN_KEY_LENGTH.

Review checklist (plan Section 9): evidence. It applies to this patch because the patched files
are byte-identical to ccaf9ee, where the S04a and S16 checks ran (records in git history at
01bdc9b):
- evidence: existing request-state tests and the Section 7 boundary tests pass (S04a, S16);
- evidence: RFC 4231 HMAC vectors and RFC 4648 Base64 vectors pass through prim (S04a);
- evidence: tokens sealed before and after are byte-identical, committed regression test mod
  byte_identity incl. Expired and InvalidKeyId (S04a); differential test base vs head, about 2.9M
  open calls, no difference (S04a review round 1; a one-off review run, not CI evidence); repeated
  for this patch: 2,463,458 results (open_at, seal, constructors) on upstream 7caec76 and on the
  patched copy, identical (D2 patch review, 2026-11-23);
- evidence: new public items documented, Aeneas-safe rules of plan Section 2.1 checked, types
  #[non_exhaustive] (S04a);
- evidence: existing public API unchanged, only addition rmcp::model::request_state_wire (rustdoc
  JSON comparison, S04a).
Reviews: S04a two independent agent reviews (no blocking) plus fix-round reviews; S16 two
independent agent reviews plus a confirming round, accepted by the human. As a translation patch
it is part of the trusted base; two independent agent reviews of it in this form found no
blocking issue (2026-11-23); signed off by the human
(Hoang Le Truong) on 2026-11-23 (VERIFICATION.md Section 4).

Size and scope: VERIFICATION.md Section 4 describes a small, one-concern patch. This one is about
3,200 lines because the D2 fallback carries the whole reviewed refactor over unchanged (byte-
identical to ccaf9ee): about 335 lines of production code (the wire module and the codec
changes) and about 2,000 lines of tests. The tests stay in the same patch because they use
items the patch creates (wire, prim), so a separate test patch could not apply without this
one, and because mod byte_identity and the RFC vectors are the evidence for the behaviour
argument above; they are #[cfg(test)] and do not change the translation.

Evidence in CI: patched-tests.yml runs the crate's library tests on the patched copy
(after scripts/aeneas-translate.sh --check-deps, cargo test --locked --no-default-features
--features request-state --lib, overflow checks and debug assertions on), which include every test
above, so a behaviour change they cover is caught.
Every target's linkage is patch-01. Delete this patch if the refactor lands in the SDK source.
